ID: B3-Ch01a-Intro
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch1_unlock,0)
 

"Hey, Barry, are you done eating?" Daren says. "It's almost time for our daily planning with Arraka."

"Yeah, I'm done," I say, setting my tin plate on the dry grass and falling in step beside him. "Let's go."

Three days have passed since the God of Time dragged us into his domain. Ollendor lies somewhere past the western ridge line; Melindra swears we will see the outer ramparts by tomorrow before noon. The trail between the mountain pass and the foothills has been quiet, foreign pine needles snapping under our boots, but nobody brings up the rituals. We talk around them. We pack the bedrolls, count the dried meat rations, and pretend the world did not tilt on its axis seventy-two hours ago. Yet beneath the mundane camp chatter, the strain is visible in every stiff shoulder and guarded glance. Selkram's trials left a mark on every single one of us.

That quiet pressure is the only reason the others agreed to my proposal: letting Arraka drill us on how to kill a god. Daren balked at first, and Hadrik laughed it off as suicide, but in the end only Leila and Melindra stayed off the official roster. Leila plans to peel off at Ollendor to reunite with her father, and Melindra's blood oath ends the moment the city's tyrant dies. Arraka's long-term schemes are not their problem.

"I still can't believe we're doing this," Daren mutters, the iron plates of his armor clanking softly as we head up the slope toward the grassy knoll above camp. "A week ago, if you told me I'd take tutoring from that banshee, I would have laughed in your face."

"We don't have a better hand to play," I say. "None of us has ever traded blows with a god. Arraka is the only one who fought them and lived to brag about it. Left to ourselves, we wouldn't even know where to strike."

"I know, I know," Daren says, tugging at the leather strap of his breastplate where it chafes his collar. "It still turns my stomach. Not so long ago she butchered an army and tried to kill us. In fact, she did kill us, before time rewound. Now she's running our morning drills? It's too neat. There's an angle in it."

"She has an ancient grudge against the entire pantheon," I say. "They exiled her from the magical plane. She wants them dead, and trapped in an amulet, we're the only weapon she can swing."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Intro2
TEXT:

"Maybe," Daren says, his hand resting on the pommel of his broadsword. "I'm still taking every word with a handful of salt. She's just as likely leading us in circles for her own entertainment."

At the crest of the hill, the rest of the company is already assembled under the pale morning sun. Leila sits cross-legged on a flat granite boulder, silver hair catching the draft, while Melindra leans against the twisted trunk of a mountain pine, watching us with idle amusement. Leila has spectated from the start, but this is Melindra's first time attending. Watching us fail to break after two days of drills must have finally piqued her curiosity.

"You're late!" Arraka's voice screeches from the golden amulet hanging against Flower's chest. "Twenty push-ups for tardiness! Cheat on the count and you'll spend the afternoon wearing a dunce hat in the briars!"

choice(""Fine, fine, I'll do the push-ups."", B3-Ch01a-Fine, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Fine)
choice(""How about you do twenty push-ups first, and I'll do mine right afterwards?"", B3-Ch01a-Afterwards, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Afterwards)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Fine
TEXT:

"Fine, fine, I'll do the push-ups," I say.

"Since when does Arraka give orders?" Daren snaps, jaw tight.

"Who cares?" I say, dropping to the dirt and knocking out the count as grit and dry needles dig into my palms. "It's just twenty push-ups in the grass. It beats standing around listening to her lecture us about punctuality."

"It's the principle of the thing," Daren grumbles, standing rigid while his scabbard taps against his greaves. "Besides, I'm not shedding eighty pounds of steel just to humor her circus act."

"Is that so?" Arraka snaps from the amulet. "What if I decide your pathetic god-slaying crusade isn't worth my charity? I'm only here out of sheer boredom. Weren't you the ones on your knees, begging for a crumb of my wisdom?"

"All I did was ask a question," I say, pushing up from the dirt and brushing the dry grass off my sleeves. "You leaped at the chance to settle your old scores before I even finished the sentence."

"Hah! Now that you mention it, you never did beg properly! Get on your knees right now. If your pleading sounds pathetic enough, I might feel generous enough to keep teaching."

"Or you could stop wasting daylight and start the lesson," Illuna says, her tone crisp and cold. "We didn't hike up here to listen to your rambling."

"Fine, fine!" Arraka says. "Take the fun out of everything. Gather round, worms. Let's see if any of you managed to accomplish anything since yesterday."

Ever since Illuna threatened to cast the amulet into the Magium, Arraka has treated her ex-disciple with an unfamiliar restraint. She would never admit it, but beneath the spite, Illuna's steel earned a grudging respect.

"Let's start with Daren," Arraka says, her tone dripping with mockery. "Tell me, hero. Have you made contact with the spirit linked to your soul? Is the count still zero? Please say zero. I could use a laugh."

"Maybe it wouldn't be zero if your instructions sounded like actual magic instead of nonsense," Daren shoots back, crossing his armored forearms. "'Untangle the flow of your aura until you can see it in your mind.' 'Picture the link as a long invisible string and give it a tug.' How is anyone supposed to work with that?"

"Only an oaf in tin plate could call my guidance nonsense!" Arraka snaps. "Do you have any idea how many mages would slaughter their own kin to have a mentor from the magical plane? Thousands of years of primordial lore, and you squander it because your brain refuses to grasp a basic metaphor!"

"I tried," Daren says, a vein pulsing at his temple. "None of it corresponds to anything I can feel."

"Then try harder! Or ask an intelligent question for once. Where are you failing? The visualization? Is your magical sense too dull to separate the layers of your own energy? Can you even read someone's age from their aura? I have no idea how blind you earthen mortals truly are. Your senses are crude, but that is no excuse for total incompetence."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Swim, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Swim)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Afterwards
TEXT:

"How about you do twenty push-ups first, and I'll do mine right afterwards?" I say.

"Mind your mouth, Barry-boy," Arraka says, her voice turning sharp. "Crack jokes like that and I might decide you're ungrateful. I'm offering my services out of pure goodwill. I have no obligation to coach insolent weaklings."

"I'm no god-slayer," I say, "but I fail to see how twenty push-ups will help me survive the God of Time."

"Is that so?" Arraka snaps. "What if I decide your pathetic god-slaying crusade isn't worth my charity? I'm only here out of sheer boredom. Weren't you the ones on your knees, begging for a crumb of my wisdom?"

"All I did was ask a question," I say. "You leaped at the chance to settle your old scores before I even finished the sentence."

"Hah! Now that you mention it, you never did beg properly! Get on your knees right now. If your pleading sounds pathetic enough, I might feel generous enough to keep teaching."

"Or you could stop wasting daylight and start the lesson," Illuna says, her tone crisp and cold. "We didn't hike up here to listen to your rambling."

"Fine, fine!" Arraka says. "Take the fun out of everything. Gather round, worms. Let's see if any of you managed to accomplish anything since yesterday."

Ever since Illuna threatened to cast the amulet into the Magium, Arraka has treated her ex-disciple with an unfamiliar restraint. She would never admit it, but beneath the spite, Illuna's steel earned a grudging respect.

"Let's start with Daren," Arraka says, her tone dripping with mockery. "Tell me, hero. Have you made contact with the spirit linked to your soul? Is the count still zero? Please say zero. I could use a laugh."

"Maybe it wouldn't be zero if your instructions sounded like actual magic instead of nonsense," Daren shoots back, crossing his armored forearms. "'Untangle the flow of your aura until you can see it in your mind.' 'Picture the link as a long invisible string and give it a tug.' How is anyone supposed to work with that?"

"Only an oaf in tin plate could call my guidance nonsense!" Arraka snaps. "Do you have any idea how many mages would slaughter their own kin to have a mentor from the magical plane? Thousands of years of primordial lore, and you squander it because your brain refuses to grasp a basic metaphor!"

"I tried," Daren says, a vein pulsing at his temple. "None of it corresponds to anything I can feel."

"Then try harder! Or ask an intelligent question for once. Where are you failing? The visualization? Is your magical sense too dull to separate the layers of your own energy? Can you even read someone's age from their aura? I have no idea how blind you earthen mortals truly are. Your senses are crude, but that is no excuse for total incompetence."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Swim, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Swim)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Swim
TEXT:

"We only know the basics, you old hag," Hadrik rumbles, crossing his thick arms over his leather vest. "We went over this yesterday."

"What passes for 'basics' in this backwater?" Arraka asks. "Discerning age and race from an aura is child's play on the magical plane. Can you manage even that much?"

"No," Hadrik says. "We gauge raw power. We use our senses to spot an enemy in the brush and tell if they're about to hurl a fireball. That's it."

"That's all?" Arraka sounds genuinely appalled. "Why didn't you say so from the start? That changes the entire curriculum! You morons need foundational drills. Breathing exercises, layered perception. How did you stumble your way to this much power without mastering the primer? Discerning an age should be effortless. Focus your magical sense on the small, spinning currents hovering above a person's head. The ones that radiate cold air like steam rising off ice. You won't catch them immediately; they're shielded by a dense cascade, like a waterfall masking a cave. And when I say look, I mean with your senses, not your eyeballs."

"That sounds even more ridiculous than yesterday's string," Hadrik says with a snort. "Where do you invent this rubbish?"

"She's leading us on," Daren says. "It's another game to—wait."

He takes a slow step toward Flower, his gaze locking onto the space just above the gold amulet.

"Did you say a waterfall?" Daren asks, brow furrowing in concentration.

"Right behind the veil," Arraka says. "Picture your focus stepping through the falling water. Reach out, grasp the spinning currents between your hands, and hold them still until the glyph settles into a number you can read."

Hadrik lets out a booming laugh that shakes his beard. "Hear that, Daren? She's telling you to take a swim!"

Daren ignores him, eyes narrowed, staring fixedly into the morning air above the amulet.

"Daren?" Hadrik prompts.

"No, this makes no sense," Daren says after a long pause, shaking his head. "It reads negative three hundred and fifteen. Is that a cipher?"

"Don't read my aura, you idiot!" Arraka snaps. "I mask my true age on purpose so prying eyes can't track my lifespan. I taught Illuna the same discipline. Pick someone else. The ice mage, or the mute girl. Neither of them hides their signatures. It should be obvious."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Nine, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Nine)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Nine
TEXT:

Daren turns toward Kate, studying the air above her brow. For several seconds the hilltop is completely still while he focuses, the morning wind rustling the dry grass around our boots.

"Kate, you're twenty-two," he says. "Is that right?"

"Yes," Kate says, her voice steady and neutral. She keeps her hands tucked inside the folds of her dark green cloak. "I never knew my parents, so I don't have an exact birth date, but that matches the estimate the Beacon gave me when they took me in."

Daren shifts his gaze to Leila. "And Leila, you're nineteen?"

Leila nods once, her expression calm.

"Barry is twenty-eight, as he claimed, and Hadrik is..." Daren's voice trails off. His eyes widen as he stares above Hadrik's head. "Wait. Something is wrong here. Your aura reads over a century old. That can't be your real age."

Hadrik's black beard splits in a wide, rumbling grin.

"The aura doesn't lie, Daren," Hadrik says. "Giants live far longer than humans. In this dwarf form I might pass for forty in human years, but I've walked this world a good deal longer."

"Right," Daren says, rubbing the back of his neck with an armored gauntlet. "I keep forgetting you're wearing a disguise."

"What about me?" Flower asks, hopping forward with an eager smile, her red hair catching the sunlight. "What does mine say?"

Daren turns his attention to the space above her red hair. His lips part, he hesitates, and then a strange look crosses his face.

"Nine," Daren says gently. "It says you're nine years old."

"No!" Flower stomps a bare foot into the turf, her face flushing with frustration. "That's wrong! It says twenty-nine! Petal, tell him!"

"The number above your head is nine," Illuna says from within, her tone flat.

"There's a two right before the nine and you know it!" Flower insists. Daren tries and fails to hide a smirk behind his hand. "I can feel the currents myself. It says twenty-nine! Why are you lying?"

choice(""I also can only see the number nine when I look above Flower's head."", B3-Ch01a-Age, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Age, v_b3_ch1_age = 0)
choice(""Stop teasing her, guys! Flower can be whatever age she wants to be."", B3-Ch01a-Age, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Age, v_b3_ch1_age = 1)
choice(""Listen, regardless of what her aura says, I think we can all agree on what her real age is."", B3-Ch01a-Age, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Age, v_b3_ch1_age = 2)
choice(""Flower, why are you so fixated upon this number? We all know for how long you've lived."", B3-Ch01a-Age, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Age, v_b3_ch1_age = 3)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Age
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch1_age == 0) {
"I also can only see the number nine when I look above Flower's head."

"You're lying!" Flower shouts, pointing an accusing finger at my nose. "You don't even have aura sense! You're just taking his side because you think it's funny!"

"Because you give him every reason to," Illuna says, taking control of the voice. "If you carried yourself with an ounce of maturity, people might treat you accordingly. As far as I can see, you remain the exact same impulsive child I met all those years ago. Not a single thing has changed."

"Fine!" Flower yells, snatching control back, her lower lip trembling into a fierce pout. "If it bothers you so much, I'll stop. I'll be serious. I'll be mature, and I'll talk like a grown-up. Will that satisfy you... Illuna?"

For a fraction of a second, the green in her eyes flashes bright sapphire. Illuna freezes in shock.

"I never asked you to use my real name when—" Illuna begins, but Flower cuts her off.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_age == 1) {
"Stop teasing her, guys! Flower can be whatever age she wants to be."

"That's right!" Flower says, puffing out her chest.

"After all," I add with an innocent shrug, "who among us didn't dream of being an adult back when we were kids?"

"I'm not playing pretend!" Flower shouts, her face burning crimson with humiliation. "I'm twenty-nine years old! Why won't anyone take me seriously?"

"Because an adult doesn't throw a temper tantrum when teased by mortals," Illuna says, taking control of the voice, her tone sharp and unyielding. "If you want them to respect your age, stop begging for their permission. You act like a child, so they treat you like one."

"Fine!" Flower yells, snatching control back, tears of frustration glistening on her eyelashes. "If it bothers you so much, I'll stop. I'll be serious. I'll be mature, and I'll talk like a grown-up. Will that satisfy you... Illuna?"

For a fraction of a second, the green in her eyes flashes bright sapphire. Illuna freezes in shock.

"I never asked you to use my real name when—" Illuna begins, but Flower cuts her off.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_age == 2) {
"Listen, regardless of what her aura says, I think we can all agree on what her real age is."

"Yeah, it's—" Flower begins, her eyes lighting up in hope, but Daren, Illuna, and I cut in simultaneously.

"Nine years old," the three of us say together.

"I am not nine!" Flower screams, her voice cracking as she stomps both feet into the turf. "You all ganged up on me! Every single one of you!"

"Nobody ganged up on you, Flower," Illuna says dryly from within. "You simply made the conclusion unanimous. If you showed even a shred of discipline, perhaps they wouldn't find it so effortless to mock you."

"Fine!" Flower yells, snatching control back, her fists clenching at her sides. "If it bothers you so much, I'll stop. I'll be serious. I'll be mature, and I'll talk like a grown-up. Will that satisfy you... Illuna?"

For a fraction of a second, the green in her eyes flashes bright sapphire. Illuna freezes in shock.

"I never asked you to use my real name when—" Illuna begins, but Flower cuts her off.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_age == 3) {
"Flower, why are you so fixated upon this number? We all know for how long you've lived."

"Because everyone still treats me like a toddler!" Flower says, her defensive fury cracking to reveal raw hurt. "Every time anyone mentions how old I am, it's the exact same joke! I was there when the cities burned, I survived the dragon wars, and people still talk to me like I'm four feet tall!"

"Surviving the centuries means nothing if you refuse to grow with them," Illuna says, taking control of the voice, her tone cooler but not unkind. "Age isn't a badge you demand from others. It is the weight you carry without shouting. Until you learn that, you will remain nine to everyone who looks at you."

"Fine!" Flower yells, snatching control back, her chin trembling as she glares at the ground. "If it bothers you so much, I'll stop. I'll be serious. I'll be mature, and I'll talk like a grown-up. Will that satisfy you... Illuna?"

For a fraction of a second, the green in her eyes flashes bright sapphire. Illuna freezes in shock.

"I never asked you to use my real name when—" Illuna begins, but Flower cuts her off.

}
"No, it is fine," Flower says, her voice dropping into a flat, measured monotone that mimics Kate's exact cadence, though her eyes glisten with hurt. "It was childish of me. I won't annoy you with that nickname anymore."

"You weren't—" Illuna begins, but Arraka's voice cuts through from the amulet.

"Take your nursery squabbling to your own bedrolls," Arraka snaps. "We didn't assemble here to listen to your 'ramblings', isn't that right, Illuna?"

"Very well," Illuna says, her expression darkening as she lets the cold mask settle over her features. "Continue the lesson."

"Thank you!" Arraka sneers. "As I was explaining before the nursery spat, Daren and Hadrik need to master genuine aura perception. The real fundamentals, not the crude senses every hedge-wizard uses to spot a campfire. You two have enough raw power that with proper guidance, you should grasp the basics in days rather than years. I'll outline the specific drills when we finish here. Daren shows some aptitude when he bothers to apply himself, but I make no promises about the dwarf. Reading auras requires a functioning intellect, and Hadrik's skull is mostly bone and tavern songs. Still, we work with what we have."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Talented, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Talented)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Talented
TEXT:

Hadrik mutters what sounds like a dwarven curse into his beard, his knuckles popping. I glance toward the amulet.

"Should the rest of us practice reading auras too?" I ask.

"No," Arraka says. "Unless you already command significant power, learning the fundamentals takes years. For you, it's a waste of breath. Kate has the strength for it, but her hours are better spent on her own drills. Illuna already knows the theory. Flower never formally studied, but being an ethereal lets her read certain signatures at a glance. And as for Leila... Leila knows more than the basics already. She reads lies directly off your aura. That's rare even among lessathi. She is operating on a plane of her own. Whether that comes from years of discipline or raw talent, I can't say."

Leila raises a hand, and glowing blue script hangs in the air between us, shimmering against the morning light.

"I'm not that talented," she writes. "Most of the time I fail to catch people's bluffs. It's only easy reads, like Barry, where I can always tell."

"The girl makes my point," Arraka says. "Barry, your bluffs are abysmal. Your face and voice might hold, but your aura trembles like a leaf in a gale the second you tell a lie. If you don't rein that in, Selkram will read you the moment we step into his presence. Get control of your energy!"

"And how am I supposed to do that?" I ask.

"Put points into your Bluff stat, obviously," Arraka says. "Do I have to explain everything?"

"My... Bluff stat?"

"The one that stabilizes your aura when you speak an outright falsehood," Arraka says. "It's called Bluff. You can't miss it."

"I don't have a Bluff stat on my device," I say. "Neither does Leila."

"What?" Arraka snaps. "Nonsense. You're misreading the display. Hand it over. Hold it close to the amulet so I can examine the particles on the screen."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Hidden, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Hidden)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Hidden
TEXT:

I pull the heavy metal device from my tunic pocket and hold it inches from the gold amulet.

The clearing falls quiet for several seconds while Arraka inspects the glowing runes humming across the casing.

"Something is wrong here," she mutters. "It's not just Bluff. Magical Sense and Aura Hardening are missing as well. They haven't been removed... they're hidden. Masked behind a dormant layer. Six centuries ago, the lessathi I observed using these contraptions had those functions unlocked manually."

"Aura Hardening?" I ask, pocketing the device. "Like the technique Hadrik uses to take a hit when he can't cast warding magic?"

"The dwarf didn't invent the wheel," Arraka says. "Warriors across centuries arrived at the same trick. Regardless, here is a task for you and Leila. If you can't break the seal on Magical Power and Knowledge, try cracking the aura stats instead. Enlist Daren's enchanting kit if needed, but don't shatter the core. We can't afford you becoming even more useless than you already are."

choice(""I'll take a look with Leila and Daren, and we'll see what we can find."", B3-Ch01a-Inconvenient, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Inconvenient, v_b3_ch1_unlock = 1)
choice(""I'd rather wait until we meet up with Leila's father, so he can have a look."", B3-Ch01a-Inconvenient, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Inconvenient, v_b3_ch1_unlock = 0)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Inconvenient
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 1) {
"I'll take a look with Leila and Daren, and we'll see what we can find," I say.

"Good," Arraka says. "Do it after we finish. Don't pull Daren away while he's working on his aura drills. Daren and Hadrik reaching their spirits comes before everything else. The rest waits."

"Why is that so critical?" Melindra asks, pushing off the tree trunk and crossing her arms. "How does talking to spirits help them fight the God of Time?"

"You missed the last two lessons," Arraka says. "Daren needs to reach his spirit to fix the ridiculous flaw in his casting: his complete inability to barrier physical attacks. The problem stems from a misaligned bond. If they realign the connection, he can ditch that clunky iron shell for proper lightweight leather. According to his old master, half of Daren's martial forms are locked away by the sheer weight of his armor. If he can use even a fraction of those techniques, our odds improve."

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 0) {
"I'd rather wait until we meet up with Leila's father, so he can have a look," I say.

"Suit yourself," Arraka says. "Keep chipping away at the cipher for your magic stats, then. That carries less risk of ruining the device. Daren can assist when he finishes his drills, but don't interrupt him. Daren and Hadrik reaching their spirits comes before everything else. The rest waits."

"Why is that so critical?" Melindra asks, pushing off the tree trunk and crossing her arms. "How does talking to spirits help them fight the God of Time?"

"You missed the last two lessons," Arraka says. "Daren needs to reach his spirit to fix the ridiculous flaw in his casting: his complete inability to barrier physical attacks. The problem stems from a misaligned bond. If they realign the connection, he can ditch that clunky iron shell for proper lightweight leather. According to his old master, half of Daren's martial forms are locked away by the sheer weight of his armor. If he can use even a fraction of those techniques, our odds improve."

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 0 || v_b3_ch1_unlock == 1) {
"And Hadrik?" Melindra asks. "What does he get out of it?"

"Nothing," Arraka says. "That isn't the point. WE gain something."

"Which is?"

"The real objective," Arraka says, her voice lowering with dark relish, "is igniting an all-out civil war between Selkram, Ulruk, and the entire magical plane."

"A civil war?" Melindra's brow lifts.

"During my conversation with Selkram, I confirmed he attempted spirit absorption to multiply his power, exactly as I did," Arraka says. "He botched it completely, of course, and never dared try again. But the other entities on the magical plane don't know that. If they realized Selkram and Ulruk attempted absorption, they would have cast them out centuries ago. The other spirits never deduced that the extra voices leaking from their speech belong to half-digested souls. If I can reach a single spirit through our trainees and expose the truth, everything collapses."

"Would anyone believe you?" Melindra asks.

"They will have no choice once I provide the proof," Arraka says. "I created the technique. I know the exact resonance tests that prove those voices are trapped spirits. Once word spreads, panic will tear through the plane. Every god and entity will move to eradicate Selkram and Ulruk. Memphir won't be able to abandon his alliance with Death, which forces the three of them into a corner against the entire realm. The God of Storms and the God of the Seas will lead the purge, accompanied by every ancient beast spirit on that plane."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Sharpen, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Sharpen)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Sharpen
TEXT:

"So you plan to have them exiled to the earthen plane?" Melindra asks, resting her chin on her hand.

"Memphir is the only one who understands the Magium well enough to attempt an exile, and after what happened last time, he won't risk it," Arraka says. "No, it will be a grinding, endless war on the magical plane. Memphir might sway a few allies, but Selkram will take a brutal beating. He'll be drained, battered, and desperate. That's when we strike."

"If the spirits are doing the heavy lifting, why are we drilling?" Hadrik asks.

"Because spirits won't finish those three easily," Arraka says. "If I couldn't obliterate them outright, lesser entities won't manage it either. The best we can expect is a stalemate that drains their reserves. Selkram will be weakened, but we still have to deliver the kill."

"Why are Daren and I the only ones trying to reach our spirits?" Hadrik asks. "Kate and Leila have sharper senses than I do."

"Reach who, genius?" Arraka snaps. "Kate is an artificial mage. She has no spirit link. Leila isn't a caster. You two are the only ones here bound to spirits on the magical plane. Flower links to Illuna, but Illuna is standing right in front of you on the earthen plane. It doesn't count."

"Can't they help us reach ours?" Hadrik asks.

"It doesn't work from the outside," Arraka says. "If external help worked, I would have dragged your spirits down myself—I can see the tether lines clear as day. Enough stalling. Kate, report. Did you sharpen your ice enough to pierce the targets I set?"

"No," Kate says, her jaw setting firmly as a faint vapor of frost clings to her knuckles. "The icicles pass straight through. I poured in as much magical power as I could muster, but nothing catches."

"Power is not the measure," Arraka says. "Sharpness is. Those targets are woven from condensed energy, identical to the solid light that forms Selkram's body. To cut energy, you have to sharpen the aura wrapping your ice, not the physical frost. A sufficiently refined aura slices pure energy regardless of velocity or mass. Power alone is useless against him."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Trembling, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Trembling)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Trembling
TEXT:

Arraka falls quiet for a moment, letting the lesson hang in the cool morning air.

"Remember," she adds, "those targets include physical matter to make them easier. Against Selkram himself, the density is pure light. Now, if Melindra joined the effort, things would move faster. Her wind blades are sharp enough to shred my targets right now. With a few days of my tutoring, she could sever solid light cleanly. What do you say, Melindra? Care to settle your score with the God of Time?"

"I told you no," Melindra says, shaking her head. "I came out of curiosity, not to sign a death warrant. I don't share your suicidal ambitions."

She pauses, her gaze drifting toward Kate's frost-chilled hands.

"I could give Kate a few pointers on refining her edges, if she wants," Melindra adds.

"Thank you," Kate says, her voice measured and guarded. "I'll take any help you're offering."

"You said aura sharpness is key," Hadrik says. "What about hardening? Can I hit Selkram with my fists if I pack enough density into my knuckles?"

"In theory," Arraka says. "In practice, far less effective. Punch Kate's targets if you want to test it, but don't expect miracles. So: Barry and Leila are tinkering with lessathi contraptions, Flower is refining her flame, Illuna is practicing my old techniques, and Daren holds the only weapon on this continent capable of cutting a god—while unable to stop a thrown pebble without eighty pounds of iron plate. I love it! This troupe is so hopelessly outmatched that if you actually kill Selkram, it will go down as the greatest humiliation in the history of both planes. The look on his face in his final moments... I tremble just imagining it!"

choice(""Yeah, I have to admit that it would be pretty funny."", B3-Ch01a-Admit, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Admit)
choice(""How about we get on with the meeting and drop the insults, for a change?"", B3-Ch01a-Insults, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Insults)
choice(""Is this the real reason why you accepted to help us? To humiliate the God of Time?"", B3-Ch01a-Humiliate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Humiliate)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Admit
TEXT:

"Yeah, I have to admit that it would be pretty funny," I say.

"And the beauty of it," Arraka says, "is that he'll never suspect you. Your sheer mediocrity is our greatest camouflage! If nobody slips up, we can dismantle his plans right under his nose."

"Is this really the grand design?" Melindra asks, raising a doubtful eyebrow. "A handful of morning drills to bring down a god?"

Arraka laughs, a sharp, echoing rasp that sends a shiver down the back of my neck.

"This is only the foundation," she says. "In the weeks ahead, I will teach these fools every flaw, quirk, and blind spot Selkram possesses. I will hand them every trick I learned across millennia of warfare. And since we must inevitably endure at least one more ritual before we can strike openly, I will teach them how to wipe that arrogant smirk off my past self's face once and for all. I'll show that buffoon what happens when she rejects my guidance and tries to leash me. Eradicated by a traveling circus—a fitting end."

"You realize you're talking about yourself, right?" Hadrik asks.

"That miserable imitation wishes she were me," Arraka sneers. "We'll see who laughs last."

"If you give us her weaknesses, aren't you giving away your own?" Hadrik asks. "Doesn't that teach us how to fight you?"

"Those vulnerabilities are five thousand years out of date," Arraka says. "I solved them long ago. Besides, if we fight again, I'd rather have a real contest than turn you to cinder in two seconds. An effortless massacre is dreadfully boring."

"We're drifting," Illuna cuts in, her tone slicing through the banter. "Arraka, what was the battle plan you mentioned on the road?"

"Right!" Arraka says. "We need absolute emotional discipline during the next ritual so Selkram gains no energy. But our efforts are meaningless if the other groups who received the prophecy feed him instead. The Sage of the West and his student were among them. Some of you met Azarius—do we have a way to reach him? A transceiver from Daren's stash?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Unfortunate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Unfortunate)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Insults
TEXT:

"How about we get on with the meeting and drop the insults, for a change?" I say.

"Did I wound your delicate pride?" Arraka mocks from the amulet. "Should I try gentler terms? Simpletons? Dunderheads? Nitwits? Pick whatever comforts you. I wouldn't want you feeling unappreciated."

"Is this really the grand design?" Melindra asks, tapping a finger against her chin. "A handful of morning drills to bring down a god?"

Arraka laughs, a sharp, echoing rasp that rattles the brass casing.

"This is only the foundation," she says. "In the weeks ahead, I will teach these fools every flaw, quirk, and blind spot Selkram possesses. I will hand them every trick I learned across millennia of warfare. And since we must inevitably endure at least one more ritual before we can strike openly, I will teach them how to wipe that arrogant smirk off my past self's face once and for all. I'll show that buffoon what happens when she rejects my guidance and tries to leash me. Eradicated by a traveling circus—a fitting end."

"You realize you're talking about yourself, right?" Hadrik asks.

"That miserable imitation wishes she were me," Arraka sneers. "We'll see who laughs last."

"If you give us her weaknesses, aren't you giving away your own?" Hadrik asks. "Doesn't that teach us how to fight you?"

"Those vulnerabilities are five thousand years out of date," Arraka says. "I solved them long ago. Besides, if we fight again, I'd rather have a real contest than turn you to cinder in two seconds. An effortless massacre is dreadfully boring."

"We're drifting," Illuna cuts in, her tone slicing through the banter. "Arraka, what was the battle plan you mentioned on the road?"

"Right!" Arraka says. "We need absolute emotional discipline during the next ritual so Selkram gains no energy. But our efforts are meaningless if the other groups who received the prophecy feed him instead. The Sage of the West and his student were among them. Some of you met Azarius—do we have a way to reach him? A transceiver from Daren's stash?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Unfortunate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Unfortunate)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Humiliate
TEXT:

"Is this the real reason why you accepted to help us? To humiliate the God of Time?" I say.

"Of course!" Arraka says, her voice ringing with vicious delight. "Slaying Selkram through a pack of mortals is exquisite revenge against his arrogance. And if the plan fails, the chaos might crack this amulet and let me finish him myself. Either way, I win."

"Is this really the grand design?" Melindra asks, watching the amulet with narrowed eyes. "A handful of morning drills to bring down a god?"

Arraka laughs, a sharp, echoing rasp that carries over the pines.

"This is only the foundation," she says. "In the weeks ahead, I will teach these fools every flaw, quirk, and blind spot Selkram possesses. I will hand them every trick I learned across millennia of warfare. And since we must inevitably endure at least one more ritual before we can strike openly, I will teach them how to wipe that arrogant smirk off my past self's face once and for all. I'll show that buffoon what happens when she rejects my guidance and tries to leash me. Eradicated by a traveling circus—a fitting end."

"You realize you're talking about yourself, right?" Hadrik asks.

"That miserable imitation wishes she were me," Arraka sneers. "We'll see who laughs last."

"If you give us her weaknesses, aren't you giving away your own?" Hadrik asks. "Doesn't that teach us how to fight you?"

"Those vulnerabilities are five thousand years out of date," Arraka says. "I solved them long ago. Besides, if we fight again, I'd rather have a real contest than turn you to cinder in two seconds. An effortless massacre is dreadfully boring."

"We're drifting," Illuna cuts in, her tone slicing through the banter. "Arraka, what was the battle plan you mentioned on the road?"

"Right!" Arraka says. "We need absolute emotional discipline during the next ritual so Selkram gains no energy. But our efforts are meaningless if the other groups who received the prophecy feed him instead. The Sage of the West and his student were among them. Some of you met Azarius—do we have a way to reach him? A transceiver from Daren's stash?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Unfortunate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Unfortunate)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Unfortunate
TEXT:

"No," Daren says. "I enchanted a supply before entering Varathia, but handing one to Azarius out of nowhere felt presumptuous. He went out of his way to travel without an entourage."

"Unfortunate," Arraka says. "I hoped for a head start. Aside from Azarius, we have no idea who else received the prophecy. Eiden was likely excluded since his actions trigger the event. Beyond that, it could be the strongest mages in the tournament, Tyrath, the golden fox, or local rulers. We need certainty, and only the time weaver can provide it."

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Rose told us she only remembered reaching Azarius and his student," I say. "Everything after that was a blur."

"She needs to dig deeper!" Arraka snaps. "She's our only scout. Starving Selkram of emotional energy isn't enough; we have to force him to burn reserves sustaining the ritual space. If the other captives don't push back in unison, the plan falls apart. And what about the time-reversal block? Is she constructing a mental wall to keep Selkram from siphoning her power?"

"Rose hasn't managed to activate her time abilities reliably since disconnecting from the Magium," I say. "She's been struggling with it for three days."

"Casting is irrelevant!" Arraka says. "Locking a door is simpler than wielding what's inside. Tell her to locate the nexus of that power in her consciousness and wall it behind an impenetrable barrier."

"I'll tell her," I say. "I can't promise she'll grasp it."

"If she survived channeling the Magium, she can build a mental ward," Arraka says. "She just needs time to reinforce it."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"The revenant told us she only remembered reaching Azarius and his student," I say. The memory of her hollow voice, drained of everything that once made Rose warm, leaves a sour chill in my throat. "Everything after that was a blur."

"She needs to dig deeper!" Arraka snaps. "She's our only scout. Starving Selkram of emotional energy isn't enough; we have to force him to burn his own power sustaining the ritual space. If the other captives don't push back in unison, the plan falls apart. Have you reached her through the transceiver?"

"Her channel has been dead since the ritual," I say. "She opens the link from Death's domain when she chooses, not the other way around."

"Damnation," Arraka growls. "If Selkram siphons her time-reversal again, fighting him is futile. What good is a lethal blow if he simply rewinds the wound?"

"We'll see her when the next ritual begins," I say. "We can explain the defense then."

"A mental barrier of that magnitude takes days of deliberate construction," Arraka says. "Springing it on her mid-ritual is cutting it perilously close."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Winner, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Winner)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Winner
TEXT:

"I'm impressed," Melindra says, resting her chin on her hand. "You put real thought into this, Arraka. I had you pegged as an improviser."

"Arraka is far more dangerous than she lets on," Illuna says, her voice cold. "Never underestimate what she can orchestrate, even trapped in an amulet."

"Don't paint me as the villain, Illuna," Arraka says. "We're all partners here! It's been centuries since I drafted a grand campaign against the pantheon. The work would move faster without so much dead weight dragging behind me, but one works with what is available."

"Hey!" Hadrik rumbles, stepping forward and clapping a heavy hand against my shoulder. "One of those dead weights is going to win this entire tournament!"

He turns toward me with a wide, toothy grin.

"Isn't that right, Barry?"

"What?... Oh. Yeah. Of course!" I say, forcing the words out a beat too late.

Kate's dark eyes narrow instantly, locking onto my face with sharp scrutiny.

"Right," Arraka says dryly from the amulet. "The champion." She lets the sarcasm linger in the cool air. "We'll cover Selkram's combat habits later. First order of business is reaching our spirit guides. Hadrik, Daren, over here. The rest of you, dismissed. We have work to do."

As the group disperses, I turn to head down the slope, but Kate steps directly into my path, her dark green cloak sweeping the grass as she blocks the trail.

choice("Try to walk past her", B3-Ch01a-Ordinary, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Ordinary, v_b3_ch1_kate1 = 0)
choice("Ask her if she can move aside", B3-Ch01a-Ordinary, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Ordinary, v_b3_ch1_kate1 = 1)
choice(""Listen, if you want to confess your feelings for me, this might not be the best place."", B3-Ch01a-Ordinary, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Ordinary, v_b3_ch1_kate1 = 2)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Ordinary
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch1_kate1 == 0) {
I step to the left. Kate mirrors my footwork with practiced ease, planting her boots in the dry turf. I step to the right; she cuts me off again, arms folded beneath her cloak.

"Are you going to let me pass?" I ask.

"No," Kate says, her voice flat and unyielding.

"Care to explain why?"

She holds my gaze in silence for several seconds before speaking, the morning breeze whipping stray strands of dark hair across her cheek.

"Why did you hesitate when Hadrik called you the winner of the tournament?" Kate asks.

"He caught me off guard," I say, trying to sidestep the question. "I was running through Arraka's drills in my head. Is pausing before an answer really worth an interrogation?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_kate1 == 1) {
"Could you move aside so I can head down?" I ask.

"No," Kate says, planting her boots firmly in the turf and crossing her arms beneath her cloak.

"Is there a reason?"

She holds my gaze in silence for several seconds before speaking, the morning breeze whipping stray strands of dark hair across her cheek.

"Why did you hesitate when Hadrik called you the winner of the tournament?" Kate asks.

"He caught me off guard," I say, trying to sidestep the question. "I was running through Arraka's drills in my head. Is pausing before an answer really worth an interrogation?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_kate1 == 2) {
"Listen, if you want to confess your feelings for me, this might not be the best place," I say.

"Your mouth still works, at least," Kate says, not blinking. "That's something. It shows parts of you are still intact. It's the parts that changed that concern me."

"Nothing changed," I say, keeping my voice light. "Same person as always."

"Then why did you hesitate when Hadrik called you the winner of the tournament?" Kate asks.

"He caught me off guard," I say, trying to sidestep the question. "I was running through Arraka's drills in my head. Is pausing before an answer really worth an interrogation?"

}
"It isn't just one pause," Kate says, her dark eyes cutting straight through me. "You've been off balance for three days. You put on the nonchalant act so the others won't question you, but the performance is paper-thin. You used to have this... stubborn blindness to impossible odds. Not courage, not fearlessness. Something weirder. Your mind refused to register the concept of mortal danger. But now you see it. You understand what death actually looks like, and it's eating you alive."

She frowns, studying my face with cold, sharp precision.

"Are we going to talk about it, or are you going to keep pretending nothing happened?"

"You see right through me, don't you?" I let out a slow breath, my shoulders slumping. "Fine. There's no point lying if you've already pieced it together. Let's walk over to those boulders. I'll tell you."

We move toward two grey granite rocks near the edge of the knoll, well out of earshot of Daren, Hadrik, and the amulet. We lean back against the rough, sun-warmed stone. Kate crosses her arms, waiting in silence.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Achieve, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Achieve)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Achieve
TEXT:

#if(v_ch7_tap == 0) {
"I should start with the cabin," I say. "Remember the story I told you about my sister Claire awakening as a banshee fifteen years ago? How she killed my parents before I could reach them?"

"I remember," Kate says.

"The memory was a lie," I say. "I learned the truth in Selkram's trial when he forced me to relive that night. I didn't arrive too late. I stood right there in the doorway, watching them die. My knees locked. The breath caught in my throat, and I froze in terror. I only moved when she turned on my younger brother—that was when I grabbed the pitchfork. I buried that memory so deep I forgot it happened. The only reason it surfaced is because I had to recreate every step to break out of the ritual."

Kate's guarded expression softens with a flicker of quiet understanding.

}
#if(v_ch7_tap == 1) {
"I should start with the cabin," I say. "Do you know the story of what happened to my sister fifteen years ago? About how she awakened as a banshee and killed my parents before I could reach them?"

"I heard you tell Melindra when she kept probing into your past," Kate says.

"The memory was a lie," I say. "I learned the truth in Selkram's trial when he forced me to relive that night. I didn't arrive too late. I stood right there in the doorway, watching them die. My knees locked. The breath caught in my throat, and I froze in terror. I only moved when she turned on my younger brother—that was when I grabbed the pitchfork. I buried that memory so deep I forgot it happened. The only reason it surfaced is because I had to recreate every step to break out of the ritual."

Kate's guarded expression softens with a flicker of quiet understanding.

}
"It went deeper than buried memories," I say, staring out over the pine canopy rolling down into the valley. "The shame of freezing broke something in how I think. I cut survival instincts out of my choices entirely so I'd never freeze again. But now that the truth is back in the open, I can't look at my own plans the same way. Every time I make a call, I wonder if it's sound judgment or just that broken recklessness talking."

"Is that why you hesitated with Hadrik?" Kate asks. "You're questioning why you entered the tournament?"

"No," I say quickly, then catch myself. "Or... maybe. I still want to become a mage. It's just..."

I trail off, searching for words against the morning breeze.

"You're no longer convinced you can do the impossible?" Kate asks.

"It's worse than that," I say. "I don't even know what the prize is anymore. The legends claim the Magium is the wellspring of all magic, but it's something vaster and far more terrifying. A stray word from Arraka nearly erased us from existence. If I won the tournament and touched it, what would I do with it? I have no way of navigating that void. One misstep is total obliteration. How am I supposed to build a life out of something that can wipe reality clean?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Solution, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Solution)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Solution
TEXT:

"Didn't Arraka say your lessathi blood gives you an innate resonance with the Magium?" Kate asks. "If her theory holds, you stand a better chance of handling it than any mage in the tournament."

"Maybe," I say. "But I thought it was a spell forge, not an existential void. Just thinking about stepping back into that expanse makes my skin crawl."

"Then don't step into it," Kate says, her tone crisp and matter-of-fact. "The answer is sitting in your pocket. Use the stat device. Unlock the magic stats, and you won't need to touch the Magium at all."

"That only lasts while I'm surrounded by mages to charge the battery," I say. "Once the tournament ends, the energy drains to zero."

"What if you use knowledge from the Magium to re-engineer the device?" Kate asks. "Arraka implied you can draw insight without physically entering the domain. Somewhere in that vast archive, there must be a method to tether your device directly to the magical plane."

"I... you're right!" I push off the boulder, the fog in my mind clearing all at once. "Why didn't I see that? I don't need to manipulate the void—I only need the blueprint. With lessathi blood and enough practice, I could build a self-sustaining device. That's brilliant!"

Kate raises an eyebrow, leaning back against the rock with her arms crossed.

"So your will to win is back?"

"Completely," I say.

"Good," Kate says, pulling her cloak tighter against the mountain wind. "Focus on the road ahead instead of drowning in your own head. Watching you mope around camp was getting exhausting."

choice(""Why, Kate, I didn't know you cared!"", B3-Ch01a-Overexerting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Overexerting, v_b3_ch1_kate2 = 0)
choice(""Was I really looking that depressed?"", B3-Ch01a-Overexerting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Overexerting, v_b3_ch1_kate2 = 1)
choice(""Wait a minute. Is this your way of saying that you were worried about me?"", B3-Ch01a-Overexerting, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Overexerting, v_b3_ch1_kate2 = 2)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Overexerting
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch1_kate2 == 0) {
"Why, Kate, I didn't know you cared!" I say.

"Don't flatter yourself," Kate says, her glare sharpening. "A useless strategist gets us all killed. Now let's head down. Leila is waiting to work on your device."

"Hold on," I say. "What about your own ritual?"

"What about it?"

"How are you holding up?" I ask. "You keep your guard up so high nobody can read you. I want to know how you're doing."

Kate looks away, her guarded posture tightening before easing slightly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_kate2 == 1) {
"Was I really looking that depressed?" I say.

"I lost count of how many times you stared blankly into the trees this morning," Kate says. "It wasn't subtle. Now let's head down. Leila is waiting to work on your device."

"Hold on," I say. "What about your own ritual?"

"What about it?"

"How are you holding up?" I ask. "You keep your guard up so high nobody can read you. I want to know how you're doing."

Kate looks away, her guarded posture tightening before easing slightly.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_kate2 == 2) {
"Wait a minute. Is this your way of saying that you were worried about me?" I say.

"I'm offering practical advice because we need you functional," Kate says, looking aside. "Nothing more. Now let's head down. Leila is waiting to work on your device."

"Hold on," I say. "What about your own ritual?"

"What about it?"

"How are you holding up?" I ask. "You keep your guard up so high nobody can read you. I want to know how you're doing."

Kate looks away, her guarded posture tightening before easing slightly.

}
As the rigid set of her shoulders relaxes, the exhaustion in her face becomes unmistakable—dark shadows beneath her eyes, the strain of sleepless nights catching up to her.

"I've had trouble sleeping," she mutters, keeping her gaze fixed on the valley below. "Nothing severe. Three days have passed; I've mostly worked through it."

"You don't look like you've worked through it," I say. "You look like you've spent seventy-two hours fighting griffins single-handed."

Kate scowls, crossing her arms tighter against her chest.

"This is why I keep my guard up," she says. "Nobody needs my burdens when everyone is carrying their own."

choice(""But I want to worry about you!"", B3-Ch01a-Approached, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Approached, v_b3_ch1_kate3 = 0)
choice(""Well, if you're sure you can handle it, then I won't press the issue any further."", B3-Ch01a-Approached, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Approached, v_b3_ch1_kate3 = 1)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Approached
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch1_kate3 == 0) {
"But I want to worry about you!" I say.

Kate blinks, caught completely off balance by the blunt declaration. For a moment the defensive edge leaves her eyes, and a faint flush rises to her pale cheeks. She looks down at the dry grass, shifting her footing.

"If you insist on fretting," she mumbles, "maybe I'll hide it slightly less when it's just the two of us." She looks back up, meeting my eyes with her usual firmness. "For now, drop it. It's something I need to settle on my own."

"Fair enough," I say. "I won't push."

"Thank you," Kate says. "Let's get back to camp. Leila has been waiting long enough."

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_kate3 == 1) {
"Well, if you're sure you can handle it, then I won't press the issue any further," I say.

Kate lets out a quiet breath, the tension in her shoulders staying locked.

"Thank you," Kate says. "Let's get back to camp. Leila has been waiting long enough."

}
We walk past the crest where Arraka continues barking instructions at Daren and Hadrik, then make our way down the grassy slope toward the tents.

Near the tree line, Leila sits on a flat rock beside a broad pine stump, her tools laid out neatly on the bark. Kate heads toward her tent, giving me a brief nod as she passes. I take the stat device from my pocket and set it on the makeshift workbench.

"Sorry for the delay," I tell Leila. "Kate wanted to talk."

Leila smiles, and bright blue letters form in the air: "No need to apologize. I'm glad you two found a quiet moment. Did she ask about your mood over the past three days?"

"How did you know? Did she say something to you?"

Leila shakes her head.

"Her eyes followed you whenever you went quiet," she writes. "Knowing Kate, a direct confrontation was inevitable. Did the conversation help?"

"Yeah," I say. "It helped a lot."

"Good," she writes. "I wasn't sure if you'd be willing to discuss the trials. Everyone has been walking on eggshells. What we lived through is difficult to speak aloud, but keeping those horrors locked inside only makes them heavier."

"Like how you shared yours the morning after the prophecy?"

Leila nods.

"I thought more people would want to talk to their friends about what they went through," she writes. "It felt natural to share."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Mechanisms, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Mechanisms) if (v_b3_ch1_unlock == 1 || v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Complicated, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Complicated) if (v_b3_ch1_unlock == 0)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Mechanisms
TEXT:

"We're still a collection of strangers who met two weeks ago," I say, gazing into the long shadows stretching between the pines. "People don't want to dump their burdens on others, and nobody wants to look fragile. If we ever talk about what the trials did to us, it will take time."

"You're right," Leila writes, the blue light fading as she lowers her hand. "Trust doesn't happen overnight... In any case, we should examine your device. Perhaps we can make some progress before turning in."

For the next two hours, Leila and I work on the stat booster beneath the trees. We start with the fine enchanting picks Daren lent us two days ago, scraping away residue and tracing the hairline conduits beneath the casing. When Daren finishes his drills on the knoll and comes down to camp, we ask for his heavier tools—the delicate picks were meant for deciphering script, not breaking into concealed registers.

With Daren's enchanting expertise and Leila's understanding of lessathi engineering, we eventually locate the dormant menu Arraka described. But unlocking the hidden stats presents a steep risk.

"No matter how I look at it, we have only one choice," Daren says, holding two thin steel styluses whose tips glow pale yellow as he probes the energy lattice around the casing. "We either force the seal or leave it alone. The problem is security. If the lessathi built an intrusion ward, forcing the register will trigger an emergency lockdown. It could lock out your standard stats until someone deactivates the failsafe. Fiddling further without proper schematics might ruin the device entirely. It might be wiser to wait for Leila's father."

choice("Leave the stat device as it is", B3-Ch01a-Crossbow, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Crossbow, v_b3_ch1_unlock = 1)
choice("Try to forcefully unlock the hidden stats.", B3-Ch01a-Crossbow, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Crossbow, v_b3_ch1_unlock = 2)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Crossbow
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch1_regrets,1) if (v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2)
achievement("No regrets",v_ac_b3_ch1_regrets)
#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 1) {
"Yeah, we shouldn't risk it," I say. "We'll continue in Ollendor."

"Sensible," Daren says, wiping the tips of his styluses with an oiled cloth and setting them into his leather tool roll. "In that case, are you ready for first watch? The camp is turning in."

"Let me grab my crossbow from my tent and I'll meet you."

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2) {
"I doubt they would ruin an entire unit over a few auxiliary stats," I say. "It isn't like the magical stats that require a cipher. Force the seal and let's see what happens."

"It's your device," Daren says reluctantly, brow furrowed. "I hope your instincts hold."

He aligns the steel picks above the housing and brings both tips down firmly against the contact nodes.

A sharp spark crackles across the metal, followed by the acrid smell of scorched insulation. The display flashes harsh crimson: 'Warning: Security Breach. Device locked for user safety. Manual reset required by authorized personnel.'

Daren looks up from the red glare, shaking his head.

"You can't say I didn't warn you. If we push any further, we risk destroying the internal arrays. We have to wait for Reidzek in Ollendor. With luck, he knows how to clear the lockout. Until then, you'll have to manage without stat boosts."

"I'll manage," I say, pocketing the glowing brick. "Nothing I haven't done before."

"First watch," Daren says, rolling up his tool kit. "The others are already asleep."

"Let me grab my crossbow from my tent and I'll meet you."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Oversee, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Oversee)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Complicated
TEXT:

We spend the next two hours working on the stat booster beneath the darkening pines, trying to crack the cipher for the two magical stats. For the first half hour Leila and I work alone with the basic picks Daren gave us, squinting at the micro-etched circuits. Once Daren comes down from the hill, he joins in, lending his enchanting sense to the effort. Unfortunately, even combined with Leila's workshop training, the cipher remains completely opaque.

"We should stop for tonight," Daren says, rotating a spiked steel ring through the air above the screen to disperse the residual charge. "Cracking a security cipher of this complexity isn't a job for a few hours on a tree stump. At this rate, we're guessing in the dark. In any case, we have first watch. The camp is turning in."

"Right," I say. "Let me grab my crossbow from my tent and I'll meet you."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Oversee, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Oversee)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Oversee
TEXT:

I bid Leila good night and duck into my tent to fetch my crossbow and quiver. Stepping back into the cool night air, I head toward the base of the hill where Daren is waiting. He sits on a low rock outcrop that gives a clear view of the clearing and the trail beyond, close enough to reach the tents in seconds if something stirs in the dark.

"Hold on, Barry," Daren says the moment I step up, his armored silhouette stark against the starlit ridge. "Stand right there for a second. Don't move."

"What is it?" I ask, freezing mid-step with the crossbow in hand.

Daren narrows his eyes, staring intently at the air around my shoulders.

"Turn around," he says.

"All right..." I turn slowly on my heel.

"Hmm," Daren mutters after several silent seconds. "Turn again, but stay in profile."

choice("Do what he says", B3-Ch01a-Backwards, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Backwards)
choice("Ask him what it is that he's trying to accomplish", B3-Ch01a-Accomplish, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Accomplish)
choice(""Want me to do a little dance too, while I'm at it?"", B3-Ch01a-Dance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Dance)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Backwards
TEXT:

I turn sideways, giving him a clear view of my profile against the dark hillside. Ten seconds pass in complete silence while the cold wind sighs through the branches above us.

"Face me again," Daren mutters, leaning forward on his armored elbows. "Take two paces forward. No, wait—back up. Walk backward slowly until I tell you to halt."

"Are you going to explain what we're doing," I ask, turning to face him, "or am I running drills until dawn?"

"Right, right," Daren says, catching himself with an embarrassed clearing of his throat. "I should have explained."

He pauses, looking slightly self-conscious.

"I'm trying to see if I can detect your lessathi blood from your aura flow," Daren says. "Arraka walked me through the principles earlier, but something isn't clicking."

"You're practicing Arraka's drills?" I stare at him. "In your free time?"

"Don't look so stunned," Daren says defensively. "I've been practicing every day."

"During scheduled group drills," I point out. "And even then, you dragged your feet like she was speaking gibberish."

"Because I thought she was inventing nonsense to mock us!" Daren says. "When she started talking about invisible strings and untangling threads, I assumed it was another cruel joke. It wasn't until I saw that waterfall signature above everyone's head that I realized the method was real."

"Funny," I say. "I thought you refused her lessons because she was evil and you suspected an ambush."

#if(v_ch11_saved_slave != 3) {
"If the rituals taught me anything," Daren says quietly, "it's that an evil creature doesn't always act out of malice. Especially someone like Eiden or Arraka. Sometimes their help is genuine, even if the motive is purely selfish. Arraka invests effort because watching a group of mortals kill a god amuses her."

He pauses, looking out into the dark pines.

"Just like Eiden gave me the power to save Ella because watching me grovel amused him, not because he had a grand scheme in mind."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_slave == 3) {
"If the rituals taught me anything," Daren says quietly, "it's that an evil creature doesn't always act out of malice. Especially someone like Eiden or Arraka. Sometimes their help is genuine, even if the motive is purely selfish. Arraka invests effort because watching a group of mortals kill a god amuses her."

He pauses, looking out into the dark pines.

"Just like Eiden gave me the power to save that slave girl because watching me grovel amused him, not because he had a grand scheme in mind."

}
