choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Humanity, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Humanity)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Humanity
TEXT:

"I'm not convinced Eiden's only motive was humiliating you," I say, leaning my weight against the rock.

"What other reason could he have?" Daren asks, his voice sharp against the night stillness.

"Maybe at first," I say. "But when you dropped to your knees and offered to do anything he asked, he didn't demand a price. He gave you the power and told you to 'prove him wrong'."

"You saw that..." Daren looks away, a grimace of embarrassment crossing his face before hardening into a scowl. "When I offered to kneel, I expected him to twist the knife. Instead he handed over the magic without asking for a thing. What did that mean?"

"Your first argument with him," I say. "In Thilias, you told him there was still good worth fighting for, and that you would right the world's wrongs if you refused to give up. That started the feud. When you threw yourself at his feet to save a single slave girl, it rattled him. He wants you to prove there is still hope for humanity—that he made a mistake giving up on mortals."

"No," Daren says, shaking his head with firm conviction. "Eiden shed his humanity centuries ago. He sees mortals as insects. He told us as much himself. He isn't having second thoughts now."

"Are you sure?" I ask. "Look at how he reacted when I vowed to save every city in Varathia. Deep down, he wants to be proven wrong, but he's too far gone to turn back. He's daring us to achieve what he abandoned."

"I'd almost forgotten your speech about saving the continent single-handed," Daren says with a weary sigh. "Winning the tournament, killing Selkram, rescuing every city—you have set yourself quite the itinerary, Barry."

"All in a day's work," I say, forcing confidence into my grin.

"Did you take a blow to the head?" Daren asks, eyeing me sharply. "There was hesitation in your voice just now."

"What hesitation? I never hesitate. You're the one hearing things."

"Right..." Daren says, narrowing his eyes. "Look, if fixing Varathia is too reckless, you don't have to carry it. The prophecy gives us a path to save the cities without banking on Eiden's whims. Counting on his goodwill is dangerous. Even if he wants to be wrong, he won't make our passage easy. He'll throw obstacles in our path to test us and entertain himself. Focus on the prophecy first. We can fix the cities once someone holds the Magium."

choice(""You're right. We already have enough things to worry about as it is."", B3-Ch01a-Uneventful, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Uneventful, v_b3_ch1_cities = 0)
choice(""I haven't changed my mind. I still want to save the cities before the tournament ends."", B3-Ch01a-Uneventful, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Uneventful, v_b3_ch1_cities = 1)
choice(""Actually, I never really wanted to save those cities in the first place."", B3-Ch01a-Uneventful, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Uneventful, v_b3_ch1_cities = 2)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Uneventful
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch1_cities == 0) {
"You're right. We already have enough things to worry about as it is," I say.

"Once we claim the Magium, setting the cities right will be straightforward," Daren says, the rigid tension in his shoulders easing. "If these troubles festered for centuries, they can wait a few more weeks."

"Leaving the cities aside, are you done using me as a target dummy? My legs are going stiff."

"A few more angles," Daren says. "The aura along your spine flows differently than the chest, and I need the right perspective."

"Hurry it up."

After another ten minutes of squinting at my aura, Daren gives up for the night, resolving to ask Arraka for clearer instructions tomorrow. We spend the remainder of the watch listening to the wind through the pines and trading stories from our lives across the sea, foreign constellations turning slowly overhead. Near dawn, Hadrik and Melindra arrive to relieve us.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_cities == 1) {
"I haven't changed my mind. I still want to save the cities before the tournament ends," I say.

"Suit yourself," Daren says, looking at me with a mix of worry and grudging respect. "Just don't let your stubbornness outpace your sword arm. If you take on the whole continent, make sure you survive the first step."

"Leaving the cities aside, are you done using me as a target dummy? My legs are going stiff."

"A few more angles," Daren says. "The aura along your spine flows differently than the chest, and I need the right perspective."

"Hurry it up."

After another ten minutes of squinting at my aura, Daren gives up for the night, resolving to ask Arraka for clearer instructions tomorrow. We spend the remainder of the watch listening to the wind through the pines and trading stories from our lives across the sea, foreign constellations turning slowly overhead. Near dawn, Hadrik and Melindra arrive to relieve us.

}
#if(v_b3_ch1_cities == 2) {
"Actually, I never really wanted to save those cities in the first place," I say.

"Whatever you say, Barry," Daren says with a dry shake of his head. "It's wiser to focus on immediate survival than grand gestures you only made to provoke a stillwater."

"Leaving the cities aside, are you done using me as a target dummy? My legs are going stiff."

"A few more angles," Daren says. "The aura along your spine flows differently than the chest, and I need the right perspective."

"Hurry it up."

After another ten minutes of squinting at my aura, Daren gives up for the night, resolving to ask Arraka for clearer instructions tomorrow. We spend the remainder of the watch listening to the wind through the pines and trading stories from our lives across the sea, foreign constellations turning slowly overhead. Near dawn, Hadrik and Melindra arrive to relieve us.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
Ever since the battle against the Sage of the North, Melindra has settled into the rhythm of the group, even volunteering for watch rotations. While her habit of baiting people for a reaction remains, the others have learned there is no malice behind the sharp tongue. Flower and I remain the ones she confides in most, but her guard around the rest has dropped noticeably.

We leave Hadrik and Melindra shuffling a deck of cards over a flat rock and head to our bedrolls.

Morning arrives with crisp mountain air and the smell of toasted travel bread cooked over Flower's fire. Once the packs are strapped, we take to the trail again, Melindra scouting ahead on light currents.

We barely clear the first ridge before Daren starts firing technical aura questions at Arraka. Stunned by the unprompted interrogation, the rest of the line slows to listen. Arraka ignores him for three paces before snapping.

"Have you lost your mind?" Arraka screeches from the amulet. "The morning lesson is over! Why are you pestering me on the trail?"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
Ever since the battle against the Sage of the North, Melindra has settled into the rhythm of the group, even volunteering for watch rotations. While her habit of baiting people for a reaction remains, the others have learned there is no malice behind the sharp tongue. Flower and I remain the ones she confides in most, but her guard around the rest has dropped noticeably.

We leave Hadrik and Melindra shuffling a deck of cards over a flat rock and head to our bedrolls.

Morning arrives with crisp mountain air and the smell of toasted travel bread cooked over Flower's fire. I pull out the transceiver to give Rose a concise rundown of Arraka's instructions regarding the mental wall and the prophecy groups. Once the call ends and the packs are strapped, we take to the trail again, Melindra scouting ahead on light currents.

We barely clear the first ridge before Daren starts firing technical aura questions at Arraka. Stunned by the unprompted interrogation, the rest of the line slows to listen. Arraka ignores him for three paces before snapping.

"Have you lost your mind?" Arraka screeches from the amulet. "The morning lesson is over! Why are you pestering me on the trail?"

}
"Because your instructions make no sense!" Daren says, throwing up his hands. "How am I supposed to isolate the lessathi flow line in Barry's aura when a thousand identical threads are shifting at once?"

"Identical?" Arraka snaps. "Every layer has its own resonance and frequency! Are you truly that dense? How much further must I simplify this before it penetrates that thick skull?"

"Start with identifying a standard lessathi aura first, Daren," Flower says, her tone uncharacteristically solemn. "Even Arraka mistook Barry for a full lessathi at first, and her senses are sharper than anyone's. If she couldn't spot the human half without looking closely, you won't catch it on your first attempt. Illuna knows how to separate lessathi and human energy. She can explain it in terms you can actually use."

"I told you not to use my real name," Illuna's voice emerges, colder. "Keep calling me Petal."

"Whatever you say, Illuna," Flower replies cheerfully.

"Ugh," Illuna mutters. "Do as you wish."

"Are you two still arguing over yesterday?" I ask.

"We aren't arguing," Flower says simply.

"Right..." I say.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Subjects, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Subjects)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Subjects
TEXT:

"The clown girl has a point," Arraka says. "Illuna tutoring Daren on lessathi auras saves my ears from his questions. And we have a genuine lessathi on hand, so he has no shortage of practice material."

"Test subject?" Leila writes, mild apprehension in her expression as the glowing blue letters float between us.

"He only needs to read your aura," I tell her. "You just stand still and turn around. At worst, you get slightly dizzy."

"I can do that," Leila writes, visibly relieved.

"Illuna," Daren asks hesitantly, "do you have a moment?"

Flower's green eyes shift sapphire as Illuna assumes control.

"Come here," Illuna says. "Let's see what you can absorb before we reach the gates."

We hike through the afternoon, the pine ridges giving way to rolling foothills of pale brush and dry soil. Daren walks alongside Illuna and Leila, concentrating on subtle shifts in the air as Leila paces ahead of him. Progress looks slow, but Daren's brow stays furrowed in genuine effort.

When Daren finally calls a halt to rest his eyes, Flower hops over to Melindra, eager to inspect the collection of colorful minerals Melindra gathered along the trail.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Infiltrate, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Infiltrate)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Infiltrate
TEXT:

