While Melindra shows Flower her polished river stones, Daren gathers the rest of us to review our infiltration plan.

"Everyone remembers the plan once we reach Ollendor?" Daren asks.

"We've gone over it five times," Hadrik grunts, adjusting his battleaxe. "If the perimeter is light, we slip through unseen. If the gate is barred, we smash the hinges. Simple enough."

}
#if(v_perception == 4 && v_ancient_languages < 4 && v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2) {
While Melindra pulls a smooth, slate-grey river stone from her pouch to show Flower, faint hairline grooves along its curved edge catch my eye. I squint at the markings, but without specialized linguistic deciphering, they look like nothing more than natural weathering.

While Melindra passes the stone to Flower, Daren gathers the rest of us to review our infiltration plan.

"Everyone remembers the plan once we reach Ollendor?" Daren asks.

"We've gone over it five times," Hadrik grunts, adjusting his battleaxe. "If the perimeter is light, we slip through unseen. If the gate is barred, we smash the hinges. Simple enough."

}
#if(v_ancient_languages == 4 && v_perception == 4 && v_b3_ch1_unlock != 2) {
As Melindra pulls a smooth, slate-grey stone from her satchel to show Flower, fine etchings along the curved edge catch my eye. Under normal circumstances I would have dismissed them as mineral veins, but the boosted linguistics modules in my stat booster translate the minute engravings instantly. Melindra turns the stone between her fingers, giving me a full view of the runes. They are carved in an archaic goblin dialect: 'The password is griffin beak.'

The phrase means little on its own, but tucked away in my memory, it might prove useful.

While Melindra puts the stone back, Daren gathers the rest of us to review our infiltration plan.

"Everyone remembers the plan once we reach Ollendor?" Daren asks.

"We've gone over it five times," Hadrik grunts, adjusting his battleaxe. "If the perimeter is light, we slip through unseen. If the gate is barred, we smash the hinges. Simple enough."

}
#if(v_ancient_languages == 4 && v_perception == 4 || v_perception == 4 && v_ancient_languages < 4 || v_b3_ch1_unlock == 2 || v_perception < 4) {
Melindra turns on her heel, arching an eyebrow. "You drafted an infiltration plan for Ollendor without asking me?"

"We asked you three times yesterday," Daren says. "You brushed us off. We couldn't wait until we were standing under the ramparts to start planning."

"Why not?" Melindra asks, looking genuinely baffled. "Wouldn't it make more sense to look at the defenses before hatching a plan?"

"We have a general sense of what to expect," Daren says. "We breached Thilias. Ollendor can't be fundamentally different."

"Ollendor is entirely different," Melindra says flatly.

"Then explain what we're facing so we can adjust," Daren says.

"It's easier to show you," Melindra says. "You'll see in five minutes."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch01a-Ending, v_current_scene = B3-Ch01a-Ending)


ID: B3-Ch01a-Ending
TEXT:

"Worst case, Melindra flies us over the walls like in Thilias," Hadrik says. "A few crossbowmen won't ground us."

"We'll clear the ridge in a few minutes and you'll have your answer," Melindra says. "Keep quiet so I can finish showing Flower these stones."

We walk in silence through the willow groves until Melindra packs her satchel. She stops before a curtain of drooping willow boughs.

"Remember during the second trial when I told you Ollendor still preserves relics from the ancient lessathi empire?" Melindra asks.

"Yeah, you mentioned that," Daren says. "Why?"

Melindra pushes the branches aside. "Look."

What we see knocks the breath from our lungs.

The valley opens into a vast stone basin, dominated by the sprawling city of Ollendor. Rising above the rooftops are the jagged black monoliths of seredium towers—the same energy-harvesting spires from the ancient empire. They are cracked and weather-worn, but they are not dormant.

Rings of purple lightning crackle chaotically around their shattered peaks, humming with contained power. Mobile platforms hover along vertical tracks, ferrying robed figures between tiers. The spires are smaller than the colossal monoliths we saw in the past—perhaps a quarter of the scale—and their energy output is far less refined, but the volume of power remains staggering.

That energy feeds a network of automated defenses. Heavy metal vehicles drift through the sky, held aloft by humming currents of magical energy. Vast glowing screens float above the plazas, flickering with images and script. Along the perimeter, towering iron golems—twenty feet tall and plated in dark steel—patrol the moat line with mechanical precision.

As we stare in stunned silence, a lone mountain crow flutters over the outer wall, gliding toward the rooftops.

The nearest golem snaps its head upward. Its iron jaws unhinge, and a lance of searing flame erupts from its maw. The bird vanishes in an instantaneous burst of white-hot heat, leaving only a puff of grey ash drifting on the wind.

Hadrik stands frozen, mouth slightly open.

"So..." Hadrik says into the dead silence. "I'm guessing flight is off the table?"

